1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
You can know your work and still struggle to explain it when the question comes. I'm Kavodel, and I built Bounty Online co-pilot to help people prepare around their own experience.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Start with your CV and the job description. The assistant connects what the role needs to the examples you have supplied. When something is missing, that should stay visible.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Every requirement is linked to evidence from the résumé. Gaps are flagged, never invented — and nothing downstream uses the brief until you approve it.

4
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Practice out loud. The question is read to you, you answer by voice, and the feedback tells you exactly what to strengthen.

5
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Interviewer: Tell me about a time stakeholders disagreed about an important metric. How did you reach a decision?

6
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:25,000
A short cue, drawn only from your approved evidence — so you can start speaking immediately, in your own words.

7
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Candidate: Product and finance were using different revenue definitions. We agreed the decision owner for each metric, and published a single model the dashboards now use. — Interviewer: What changed after that decision, and how did you know it worked?

8
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:55,000
When your profile has nothing, it says so — and offers a truthful bridge instead of inventing one.

9
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Here is what to carry into your next practice — saved as your next action.

10
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:35,000
The same co-pilot turns your script into a teleprompter that follows your voice, holds when you pause, and bridges you back when you wander.

11
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:45,000
The reports nobody reads become voiceovers people actually finish.

12
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:55,000
I used Meta's Muse Code to help build the application.

13
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Bounty Online co-pilot. Bring your experience into the moment.
